Unit Speed Test Support Implementation
======================================

Source proposal: the accepted `ds.setUnitSpeed` design from the current
conversation, informed by DFHack's `fastdwarf` implementation and DwarfSpec's
existing per-example game-state and cleanup contracts.

Purpose:
    ☐ Add a small, explicit `ds.setUnitSpeed(options)` API for end-user live tests.
    ☐ Let one example accelerate supported unit action timers, teleport eligible
      units to current-job destinations, or request both behaviors.
    ☐ Keep behavior selection visible in test code through `fast_actions`,
      `teleport_jobs`, and optional `unit_ids` fields.
    ☐ Own, stop, and verify the recurring accelerator through DwarfSpec's normal
      per-example cleanup path without claiming to reverse gameplay progression.

Completion contract:
    ☐ Treat this checklist and the accepted conversation design as the
      implementation contract; stop and reconcile them before implementing any
      disputed behavior.
    ☐ Keep every task unchecked until its implementation and stated evidence are
      complete.
    ☐ Complete production changes, focused unit tests, declaration checks,
      documentation, package verification, focused live automation, cleanup proof,
      and a final proposal-to-implementation audit.
    ☐ Keep source, unit, declaration, integration, package, and live-runtime
      evidence distinct; do not substitute one evidence class for another.
    ☐ Preserve unrelated worktree and index changes; do not stash, commit, reset,
      or overwrite them.
    ☐ Add language-standard documentation comments to every new or changed class,
      public method, internal method, and callable module API.
    ☐ Perform an implementation review against this checklist before marking any
      phase complete.

Accepted public contract:
    ☐ Name the public command `ds.setUnitSpeed(options)` and the declaration type
      `dwarfspec.UnitSpeedOptions`.
    ☐ Define `fast_actions? boolean`, `teleport_jobs? boolean`, and
      `unit_ids? integer[]` as the complete first-version option surface.
    ☐ Require at least one behavior flag to be `true`.
    ☐ When `unit_ids` is omitted, snapshot the currently eligible citizen and
      long-term-resident unit ids at activation instead of dynamically adding later
      arrivals.
    ☐ When `unit_ids` is present, require a nonempty array of unique valid ids whose
      units are eligible at activation; retain ids rather than borrowed unit objects.
    ☐ Apply the configured behavior only to the captured target ids and re-resolve
      each unit before every update.
    ☐ Skip a captured target without mutation whenever it is absent or no longer
      satisfies the accepted eligibility predicate; reserve asynchronous failure for
      adapter errors and violated native invariants.
    ☐ When both behaviors are enabled, attempt job teleportation before applying
      fast action timers to the same target.
    ☐ Reject a second `setUnitSpeed` activation in the same example while the first
      remains active.
    ☐ Return no value from `ds.setUnitSpeed(options)`.
    ☐ Keep `ds.setGameSpeed(tps)` as the separate simulation-TPS command; do not
      change its behavior or overload it with unit-speed options.
    ☐ Do not implicitly unpause the game. Tests that need simulation advancement
      continue to call `ds.setGamePaused(false)` explicitly.

Scope boundaries:
    ☐ Reproduce the relevant fastdwarf mode-1 action acceleration and job-travel
      teleportation through DFHack Lua APIs.
    ☐ Do not invoke, enable, disable, configure, or parse output from the
      `fastdwarf` plugin.
    ☐ Do not read or write `fastdwarf/config` persistent site data.
    ☐ Do not expose or modify DF's process-global `debug_turbospeed` flag and do not
      implement fastdwarf mode 2.
    ☐ Do not add command-line switches, Busted-name tags, project-wide defaults,
      named presets, arbitrary filters, or a general background-task public API.
    ☐ Do not dynamically include migrants, visitors, hostiles, animals, or other
      units that were not captured as eligible targets at activation.
    ☐ Do not promise restoration of action timers, positions, paths, completed
      jobs, produced items, RNG state, needs, or other gameplay consequences.
    ☐ Do not treat removal of the recurring accelerator as proof that the loaded
      fortress was restored.
    ☐ Keep managed-world fixture integration limited to a documented future seam;
      the current world-mount document remains exploratory rather than shipped
      runtime behavior.

Assumptions and open questions:
    ☐ Assume the recurring accelerator runs once per unpaused simulation tick, not
      once per raw render frame, so a paused test does not teleport units or rewrite
      timers. Confirm this cadence with live characterization before freezing it.
    ☐ Assume explicit `unit_ids` must identify the same citizen/resident population
      accepted by the default scope. Record and resolve any concrete need for
      noncitizen targets before broadening the contract.
    ☐ Use the actual current `fastdwarf.cpp` teleport guards as the behavioral
      baseline. Treat documentation-only claims about pushing or being followed as
      unconfirmed until native state and Lua-visible fields are characterized.
Phase 0: Freeze the behavior map and baseline
    ☐ Exit with every accepted behavior mapped to current production, declaration,
      test, documentation, package, and live-runtime seams, plus recorded pre-change
      evidence.

0.1 Contract inventory:
    ☐ Map `setUnitSpeed`, `UnitSpeedOptions`, both behavior flags, optional target
      ids, one-active-controller ownership, pause independence, and automatic
      deactivation to checklist rows below.
    ☐ Record the difference between game TPS, supported action-timer acceleration,
      job-destination teleportation, and excluded native global turbo speed.
    ☐ Record that activation cleanup is reversible but gameplay effects are not.
    ☐ Record the default and explicit targeting rules, including snapshot timing,
      duplicate handling, eligibility validation, and later unit re-resolution.
    ☐ Record the actual fastdwarf teleport guards and the state changed by
      `dfhack.units.teleport()` and path clearing.

0.2 Repository seam map:
    ☐ Confirm `src/dwarfspec/driver/commands/game_state.lua` or a focused sibling
      command module as the public command-binding seam.
    ☐ Assign target selection, action mutation, teleport eligibility, controller
      state, and update execution to focused modules beneath
      `src/dwarfspec/driver/simulation/` if a split remains justified after review.
    ☐ Confirm `src/dwarfspec/host/execution/run_capabilities.lua` as the boundary for
      any host-owned recurring scheduling, cancellation, verification, and
      asynchronous-failure capability exposed to the driver.
    ☐ Confirm `src/dwarfspec/ds.lua` as composition only and avoid adding another
      inline implementation alongside the driver command binding.
    ☐ Map public annotations to `src/ds.d.lua` and reserve the new command in
      `src/dwarfspec/protocol/configuration/schema.lua`.
    ☐ Map focused tests to `tests/unit/driver`, run-capability tests to
      `tests/unit/host/execution`, declaration fixtures to `tests/declarations`, and
      live coverage to `tests/automation`.
    ☐ Map user documentation to `docs/writing-tests.md`, command summaries to
      `docs/command-line.md` where applicable, architecture ownership to
      `docs/architecture.md`, and release notes to `CHANGELOG.md` when release policy
      requires them.

0.3 Baseline evidence:
    ☐ Record `git status --short` and distinguish pre-existing work from this task.
    ☐ Run the focused game-state, run-capability, cleanup, schema, and declaration
      unit suites and record exact success, failure, error, and pending counts.
    ☐ Run the full unit suite and record its baseline result without weakening later
      gates for any pre-existing failure.
    ☐ Run `tools/Check-Lua.ps1` and both valid and invalid
      `tools/Check-SourceDeclarations.ps1` modes and record baseline results.
    ☐ Record the established focused and complete live-automation commands and the
      package publication and inspection procedure before changing production files.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Every proposal obligation and exclusion has an implementation or evidence
      owner, unresolved native behavior is isolated behind characterization, and
      baseline evidence is recorded before production changes.

Phase 1: Characterize native unit-speed behavior
    ☐ Exit with the supported Lua operations, tick cadence, unit eligibility, and
      teleport guards proven against the current DFHack/DF version before building
      the public controller.

1.1 Action-timer characterization:
    ☐ Verify the current Lua call shape for
      `dfhack.units.setGroupActionTimers(unit, 1,
      df.unit_action_type_group.All)`.
    ☐ Prove it changes supported positive action timers to one without changing
      unsupported action records or nonpositive timer values.
    ☐ Identify the action types that expose mutable timers through the current
      DFHack binding and record the distinction between supported and unsupported
      members of the `All` group.
    ☐ Confirm the operation can be applied from DwarfSpec's in-process coroutine
      without invoking a plugin command.

1.2 Target-population characterization:
    ☐ Verify the current Lua citizen query includes active living citizens and
      long-term residents and determine its treatment of insane units.
    ☐ Define one eligibility predicate used for both default capture and explicit-id
      validation.
    ☐ Verify that retaining ids and resolving `df.unit.find(id)` on later updates is
      safe across unit disappearance, death, inactivity, and world unload, and prove
      that absent or newly ineligible targets are skipped without mutation.
    ☐ Confirm a later migrant or resident is not added after the activation snapshot.

1.3 Teleport characterization:
    ☐ Verify Lua access to dragger and draggee relationship ids, `following`,
      unconscious counters, current job, current position, path destination,
      pathability, destination visibility, teleportation, and path clearing.
    ☐ Prove the safe no-op cases for dragging or being dragged, following another
      unit, unconsciousness, no current job, invalid or identical positions,
      disconnected pathability groups, unrevealed destinations, and failed teleport.
    ☐ Determine whether pushing and being followed have reliable distinct native
      signals; do not claim or implement those extra guards without evidence.
    ☐ Confirm successful teleportation updates native position and occupancy through
      `dfhack.units.teleport()` and only then clears the remaining path.
    ☐ Record that an occupied destination is not an additional DwarfSpec guard:
      preserve DFHack's teleport-helper behavior, including forcing an arriving unit
      onto the ground when another unit is already standing at the destination.
    ☐ Record the risks of riders, projectile state, occupancy updates, and other
      side effects handled by DFHack's teleport helper.
    ☐ Confirm the fastdwarf-compatible combined order: attempt job teleportation
      before applying fast action timers to the same target.

1.4 Scheduling characterization:
    ☐ Prove a one-simulation-tick recurring callback does not run while paused and
      resumes when simulation advances.
    ☐ Prove cancellation prevents future invocations even when cleanup occurs from
      assertion failure, command timeout, explicit abort, or world unload.
    ☐ Identify the supported native verification signal that proves no callback
      remains registered.
    ☐ Characterize callback exceptions and select a bounded path that reports them
      to the active DwarfSpec run instead of silently logging or repeating them.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Every native call, eligibility rule, cadence assumption, cancellation signal,
      and asynchronous-failure path required by the controller has current live
      evidence or an explicitly reconciled contract change.

Phase 2: Add a run-owned recurring-operation capability
    ☐ Exit with a narrow internal capability that one driver controller can use to
      schedule, cancel, verify, and fault a recurring operation without importing host
      modules or exposing a public scheduler API.

2.1 Host scheduling adapter:
    ☐ Add a host-owned adapter for recurring simulation-tick callbacks using the
      current supported DFHack mechanism.
    ☐ Retain the opaque handle returned by the scheduler as the authoritative
      cancellation and scheduled-state identity; treat any human-readable label as
      diagnostic metadata rather than a global ownership key.
    ☐ Register ownership before the first callback can mutate unit state.
    ☐ Make cancellation idempotent and expose a read-only scheduled-state probe.
    ☐ Guard every callback with active-run and controller-generation checks so a
      late callback becomes a no-op.
    ☐ Convert callback exceptions into one bounded active-run failure and stop the
      recurring operation before further callbacks occur.

2.2 Capability injection:
    ☐ Extend `run_capabilities.lua` with only the scheduling, cancellation,
      scheduled-state, and failure-reporting operations required by unit-speed
      control.
    ☐ Keep the capability private to driver composition; do not export it on `ds`.
    ☐ Preserve the rule that driver modules import no module beneath
      `dwarfspec.host`.
    ☐ Inject deterministic in-memory scheduler doubles for focused driver tests.

2.3 Cleanup integration:
    ☐ Register recurring-operation cleanup before scheduling the first callback.
    ☐ Stop the controller, cancel its callback, clear retained ids and state, and
      verify the schedule is absent during cleanup.
    ☐ Continue later LIFO cleanup entries after a cancellation failure while
      retaining the primary cleanup error.
    ☐ Expose `unit_speed_active` through the authoritative run cleanup probe.
    ☐ Make final cleanup confirmation fail if the unit-speed controller, callback,
      or ownership token remains active.

2.4 Focused verification:
    ☐ Prove scheduling, opaque-handle cancellation, idempotence, generation guards,
      and read-only verification with injected in-memory doubles.
    ☐ Prove cleanup after success, assertion failure, command timeout, explicit
      abort, world unload, scheduling rejection, cancellation rejection, and callback
      failure.
    ☐ Prove a callback failure is reported once and cannot recur into a later
      example or run.
    ☐ Prove no process-global recurring entry remains after every terminal path.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ The driver has a tested recurring-operation capability whose lifecycle and
      fault behavior are fully owned and observable by DwarfSpec cleanup.

Phase 3: Implement option normalization and unit targeting
    ☐ Exit with an immutable per-example configuration and stable target-id set that
      later update behaviors can consume without reinterpreting public input.

3.1 Option validation:
    ☐ Require one table argument and reject unknown fields.
    ☐ Require `fast_actions` and `teleport_jobs`, when present, to be booleans.
    ☐ Reject configurations where neither behavior is `true`.
    ☐ Require `unit_ids`, when present, to be a nonempty dense array of integer ids;
      define validity by successful unit resolution and activation-time eligibility
      instead of imposing a separate positive-number range.
    ☐ Reject duplicate ids with a diagnostic that identifies the duplicate.
    ☐ Copy and freeze normalized options before scheduling work so caller mutation
      cannot change an active example.

3.2 Target capture:
    ☐ Implement one injected target adapter for enumerating default eligible units,
      resolving explicit ids, and re-resolving ids on updates.
    ☐ Snapshot default targets exactly once during activation in deterministic id
      order.
    ☐ Validate every explicit id before owning or scheduling the controller and
      report invalid and ineligible ids together in bounded diagnostics.
    ☐ Store only copied ids and normalized behavior flags in controller state.
    ☐ Apply the characterized later-lifecycle policy consistently when a captured
      unit becomes unavailable or ineligible.

3.3 Ownership rules:
    ☐ Reject a second activation while one controller is active in the example.
    ☐ Allow a later example to create a fresh controller only after cleanup proved
      the prior controller inactive.
    ☐ Fail before mutation if no world, map, or fortress site required by the
      operation is loaded.
    ☐ Leave pause state and TPS unchanged during validation and activation.

3.4 Focused verification:
    ☐ Cover every valid flag combination and every unknown, missing, mistyped,
      empty, sparse, duplicate, invalid, and ineligible input case.
    ☐ Prove caller tables and id arrays are copied and later mutations have no
      effect.
    ☐ Prove default snapshot ordering, explicit subset behavior, no later-arrival
      expansion, id re-resolution, and one-active-controller rejection.
    ☐ Prove every validation failure occurs before cleanup ownership, scheduling,
      timer mutation, or teleportation.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ One immutable validated configuration and deterministic target-id set enters
      the controller, and invalid input cannot create partial runtime state.

Phase 4: Implement fast action timers
    ☐ Exit with targeted fastdwarf mode-1-equivalent action acceleration driven by
      the run-owned controller.

4.1 Update behavior:
    ☐ On each scheduled simulation tick, re-resolve captured targets in stable id
      order.
    ☐ When `fast_actions=true`, call the characterized DFHack group-action-timer API
      with amount `1` and group `All` for each eligible target.
    ☐ Do not mutate action timers when `fast_actions=false`.
    ☐ Do not modify unit attributes, skills, jobs, needs, labor assignments, game
      TPS, or native global turbo speed directly.
    ☐ Preserve callback fault containment when an adapter or native invariant fails.

4.2 Focused verification:
    ☐ Prove exactly one action-timer call per eligible captured target per scheduled
      update.
    ☐ Prove stable target ordering and skips for targets that follow the accepted
      later-lifecycle policy.
    ☐ Prove the disabled flag produces no action mutation while teleportation can
      remain active independently.
    ☐ Prove cleanup stops all later action-timer calls without claiming to restore
      timers already changed.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Captured eligible units receive supported one-tick actions only while their
      example owns an active fast-action controller.

Phase 5: Implement job-destination teleportation
    ☐ Exit with targeted fastdwarf-equivalent job travel guarded by explicit native
      eligibility and safe path checks.

5.1 Teleport decision:
    ☐ Re-resolve the unit and its current job and destination within the same update
      that attempts teleportation.
    ☐ Skip dragging and dragged units, units following another unit, unconscious
      units, units without current jobs, invalid positions or destinations, and units
      already at the destination.
    ☐ Require `dfhack.maps.canWalkBetween(current, destination)` and a visible
      destination before teleporting.
    ☐ Do not infer safety from path existence alone and do not reveal a destination
      for the test.
    ☐ Call `dfhack.units.teleport()` exactly once for an eligible target.
    ☐ Clear the unit's remaining path only after teleportation reports success.
    ☐ Leave position and path unchanged when teleportation reports failure.

5.2 Combined behavior:
    ☐ When both flags are enabled, attempt teleportation before applying fast-action
      timers to the same target.
    ☐ Prove each behavior remains independently selectable.
    ☐ Keep one target's ordinary skip from preventing later targets from being
      processed.
    ☐ Route invariant or adapter failures through the controller fault path rather
      than silently treating them as ordinary eligibility skips.

5.3 Focused verification:
    ☐ Cover every teleport guard independently with in-memory unit, map, and DFHack
      adapter doubles.
    ☐ Prove visible reachable destinations teleport and clear the path after success.
    ☐ Prove disconnected, hidden, invalid, or failed destinations follow the
      accepted contract without unsafe fallback.
    ☐ Prove an occupied destination preserves DFHack teleport-helper behavior rather
      than introducing a DwarfSpec-specific rejection guard.
    ☐ Prove combined action and teleport calls have deterministic ordering and
      cleanup stops both behaviors.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Job teleportation occurs only for captured units satisfying every accepted
      guard, and failure paths do not clear routes or leak recurring work.

Phase 6: Bind the public API and declarations
    ☐ Exit with one documented `ds.setUnitSpeed` entry point composed through the
      existing driver boundary and visible to source and installed-package users.

6.1 Public composition:
    ☐ Load and bind the focused unit-speed command from `src/dwarfspec/ds.lua`.
    ☐ Supply only injected controller, target, native-operation, recurring-task,
      cleanup, and failure-reporting dependencies required by the command.
    ☐ Avoid adding an inline duplicate implementation in the root `ds.lua` facade.
    ☐ Reserve `setUnitSpeed` against project command collisions.
    ☐ Preserve all existing `ds` commands and project extension behavior.

6.2 Declaration surface:
    ☐ Add `dwarfspec.UnitSpeedOptions` to `src/ds.d.lua` with the three accepted
      fields and precise default and targeting documentation.
    ☐ Add `DS.setUnitSpeed(options)` with documentation distinguishing unit action
      speed from `setGameSpeed` TPS.
    ☐ Define `DS.setUnitSpeed(options)` as a void command with no declared return
      value; assigning its Lua result therefore yields `nil`.
    ☐ State that cleanup disables the accelerator but does not reverse gameplay
      effects.
    ☐ Add valid declaration coverage for omitted and explicit `unit_ids` and both
      independent behavior flags.
    ☐ Add invalid declaration coverage for unknown fields, wrong flag types, and
      invalid id element types.

6.3 Public-contract verification:
    ☐ Prove `ds.setUnitSpeed` is present only during an active run and is unavailable
      to conflicting custom command registration.
    ☐ Prove source-tree and installed-package composition load the same focused
      implementation.
    ☐ Prove the declaration surface matches runtime names and option fields and that
      runtime and declaration surfaces both expose void return behavior.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ End-user specs can call one typed `ds.setUnitSpeed(options)` command without
      accessing internal scheduling, native adapters, or the fastdwarf plugin.

Phase 7: Qualify lifecycle and live behavior
    ☐ Exit with focused native evidence for action acceleration, teleportation,
      selection, pause behavior, failure cleanup, and consecutive-run isolation.

7.1 Focused live fixture design:
    ☐ Use a controlled disposable fortress fixture and identify target units and
      jobs deterministically; do not depend on arbitrary player fortress state.
    ☐ Keep the focused scenario bounded and avoid long organic waits.
    ☐ Capture enough native state to prove the target action timer or job transition,
      destination, target scope, and absence of changes to a control unit.
    ☐ Register any fixture-specific restoration and verification before mutating
      native assignments or positions.
    ☐ Do not save mutated gameplay state as part of the test.

7.2 Live behavior evidence:
    ☐ Prove `fast_actions=true` advances a supported target action without changing
      game TPS or pause state.
    ☐ Prove `teleport_jobs=true` moves an eligible target to its current visible,
      reachable job destination and clears its remaining path.
    ☐ Prove explicit `unit_ids` excludes an otherwise eligible control citizen.
    ☐ Prove an omitted target list uses the activation snapshot and does not add a
      later eligible unit.
    ☐ Prove disabled behavior flags have no corresponding native effect.
    ☐ Prove a paused simulation performs no recurring unit-speed update until the
      test explicitly unpauses it.

7.3 Terminal cleanup evidence:
    ☐ Run a passing scenario and confirm `unit_speed_active=false` and
      `cleanup_confirmed=true` in the terminal report.
    ☐ Run a controlled assertion-failure scenario and prove the accelerator is still
      cancelled and cleanup is confirmed even though the test result fails.
    ☐ Run the focused suite consecutively and prove the second run inherits no
      callback, target ids, controller fault, fastdwarf setting, or native turbo flag
      from the first.
    ☐ Confirm the service ends with an idle executor, empty queue, no quarantine,
      responsive DFHack process, and no owned recurring task.
    ☐ Keep gameplay-restoration evidence separate from accelerator cleanup; do not
      report the world pristine unless the fixture-specific verifier proves it.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Focused live runs prove both behaviors and all terminal paths end with verified
      accelerator cleanup, bounded execution, and no cross-example or cross-run leak.

Phase 8: Documentation, package, regression, and final audit
    ☐ Exit with the feature documented, packaged, regression-tested, and audited
      against every accepted requirement and exclusion.

8.1 User documentation:
    ☐ Document individual-test and suite-wide `before_each` examples in
      `docs/writing-tests.md`.
    ☐ Document all option defaults, eligibility rules, pause independence,
      one-active-controller rule, and explicit target behavior.
    ☐ Clearly distinguish `setUnitSpeed` from `setGameSpeed` and fastdwarf mode 2.
    ☐ Warn that cleanup removes the accelerator but cannot rewind gameplay and
      recommend controlled disposable or managed-world fixtures.
    ☐ Document that Busted tags and DwarfSpec CLI flags select tests but do not
      enable unit-speed behavior.
    ☐ Update architecture and public-command summaries without presenting excluded
      future extensions as shipped behavior.

8.2 Regression verification:
    ☐ Run focused unit suites for the recurring capability, unit-speed controller,
      command binding, cleanup, schema reservation, and declarations.
    ☐ Run the complete unit suite and record exact success, failure, error, and
      pending counts.
    ☐ Run `tools/Check-Lua.ps1` and record the exact checked-file count.
    ☐ Run valid and invalid `tools/Check-SourceDeclarations.ps1` modes and verify the
      expected absence or exact presence of diagnostics.
    ☐ Run `git diff --check` and inspect the scoped diff for unrelated changes.

8.3 Package verification:
    ☐ Run `tools/Publish.ps1` through the established package workflow.
    ☐ Inspect the generated rock and verify every new production module,
      declaration, and required documentation artifact is present at the expected
      path.
    ☐ Install or inspect the exact generated artifact according to the established
      release checklist and prove `ds.setUnitSpeed` resolves from the installed layout.
    ☐ Re-run the focused installed-package contract without falling back to source
      files from the checkout.

8.4 Final implementation audit:
    ☐ Map every accepted public-contract, scope-boundary, cleanup, fault,
      documentation, and verification row to implemented source and evidence.
    ☐ Confirm there is no CLI flag, Busted tag behavior, project default, global
      turbo mode, fastdwarf persistent-data mutation, or unsupported target expansion.
    ☐ Confirm every new or changed method, class, and callable module API has the
      required documentation comment.
    ☐ Confirm cleanup proof refers only to owned accelerator state and does not imply
      world restoration.
    ☐ Perform the required follow-up code review and reconcile every discrepancy
      before checking final acceptance.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Source, unit, declaration, package, and focused live evidence independently
      support the shipped `setUnitSpeed` contract, and the final audit finds no
      unresolved proposal drift or cleanup gap.

Final acceptance:
    ☐ `ds.setUnitSpeed(options)` is the only new public command and accepts exactly
      `fast_actions`, `teleport_jobs`, and optional `unit_ids`.
    ☐ `ds.setUnitSpeed(options)` returns no value.
    ☐ Fast actions and job teleportation are independently and jointly usable for
      the captured eligible target ids.
    ☐ The command neither changes pause/TPS implicitly nor touches native global
      turbo speed or fastdwarf configuration.
    ☐ Every success, failure, timeout, abort, and callback-fault path stops and
      verifies the recurring accelerator before cleanup can be confirmed.
    ☐ Documentation accurately distinguishes reversible accelerator ownership from
      irreversible gameplay effects.
    ☐ All required focused, full, declaration, syntax, package, installed-layout,
      live-runtime, repeatability, cleanup, and final-review evidence is recorded.
